# Block Ownership Model, Phase 7 (Fork C): a bounded static analysis
# proving, for SOME box_set call sites, that the target can only ever have
# refcount 1 -- letting lower.patlang emit BoxSetUnchecked (skip the
# runtime check entirely) instead of BoxSet (Phase 3's runtime fallback).
#
# Scope, kept deliberately narrow and stated plainly rather than
# oversold: this tracks a `fresh` set (names PROVEN uniquely owned right
# now) through a STRAIGHT LINE of top-level statements in ONE function
# body ONLY -- the moment anything this analysis can't reason about
# precisely is reached (an if/else, a while, a jump to another block), the
# ENTIRE set is invalidated rather than reasoned about further. This is
# maximally conservative, not a general points-to analysis (section 9's
# own non-goal) -- it proves the easy, common case (create a box, mutate
# it a few times in the same straight-line block, before anything else
# happens) and honestly falls back to Phase 3's runtime check for
# everything else, exactly matching Fork C's own hybrid resolution
# (docs/plans/block-ownership-model.md, section 6): prove what's provable,
# never reject a program outright, never claim more than what's actually
# checked.
#
# What this does NOT attempt, named rather than silently absent: proving
# anything about a value that arrived as a PARAMETER (the caller's own
# aliasing state is invisible to a single block's own local analysis --
# exactly the kind of cross-block reasoning Fork C's own downside #4
# names as close to research territory in general); an equivalent of
# "run_ir" firing the flight check again for a dynamically-compiled
# fragment (this engine has no such dynamic-compilation entry point at
# all -- there is nothing to wire this into for that case, not something
# skipped).
#
# What this DOES prove, soundly: a box_set on a name is safe to leave
# UNCHECKED if that name was bound, in this same straight-line run, by
# `let X = box_new(...)` or `let X = box_set(...)` (a box_set's own result
# is ALWAYS a uniquely-owned pointer, whether it mutated in place or
# cloned -- either way nothing else could hold that exact returned
# pointer yet), and has not been used as a jump-call argument or passed to
# box_share since.

make a function called bm_fc_list_without takes xs, v returns out
  let n = list_len(xs)
  let result = []
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let item = list_get(xs, i)
    if item != v then
      let result = list_push(result, item)
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return result
end

make a function called bm_fc_contains takes xs, v returns yes
  let n = list_len(xs)
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    if list_get(xs, i) == v then
      return true
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return false
end

make a function called bm_fc_is_provably_unique takes fresh, name returns yes
  return bm_fc_contains(fresh, name)
end

# Updates `fresh` after lowering `let name = value_node`. box_new/box_set
# results are always uniquely owned right after being bound (see this
# file's own header); box_share's result never is, and its OWN argument
# (the box being shared) is genuinely no longer provably unique either --
# a real second live reference now exists via the returned pointer, and
# bm_rc_inc really did bump the header count, so failing to invalidate it
# here would be a new, real unsoundness, not an inherited one. Anything
# else (an ordinary value, a plain `let c = b` copy) just clears whatever
# `name` may have meant before.
#
# Named limitation, INHERITED from lower.patlang's own Phase 3/4 design,
# not introduced here: a plain `let c = b` copy of an already-fresh box
# pointer does not remove `b` from `fresh`, because it doesn't remove
# it from being usable as a refcount-1 mutation target under the RUNTIME
# check either -- lower.patlang's own header already discloses that
# automatic refcounting on every value copy was never built (box_share is
# the one explicit op that does), so a later box_set(b, ...) mutating in
# place while `c` silently aliases it is a pre-existing gap in what the
# runtime check itself can catch, not something this analysis makes
# worse. This flight check's own soundness claim is relative to that
# already-disclosed baseline: whatever the runtime check would also have
# concluded, this analysis is not permitted to disagree with in the
# unsafe direction.
make a function called bm_fc_after_let takes fresh, name, value_node returns out
  let fresh = bm_fc_list_without(fresh, name)
  if list_get(value_node, 0) == "Call" then
    let callee = list_get(value_node, 1)
    let args = list_get(value_node, 2)
    if (callee == "box_new") or (callee == "box_set") then
      return list_push(fresh, name)
    elif callee == "box_share" then
      if list_len(args) > 0 then
        let arg0 = list_get(args, 0)
        if list_get(arg0, 0) == "Var" then
          return bm_fc_list_without(fresh, list_get(arg0, 1))
        end
      end
      return fresh
    end
  end
  return fresh
end
